 'dt.balustrade':'Setback edge protection',
 'dt.note.drained-cavity':'The air seal is continuous on the inside and the pressure is held equal across it; water that gets past the outer seal is collected and drained out. Air that leaks is a heating and cooling bill, so the number is reported against the 0.2 L/(s·m²) code limit.',
 'dt.note.thermal-break':'A low-conductivity isolator breaks the metal path from the outside pressure plate to the inside mullion frame. It roughly halves the U-factor against the same curtain wall without it, and it is the single largest thermal decision available on this wall.',
 'dt.note.slab-edge':'The weight-bearing bracket and the slip connection are different connections doing different jobs. The floor plate deflects, the frame drifts and the metal moves with temperature; the anchor has to permit all three while still carrying the wall. Beyond about 4.1 m the deflection allowance is L/240 plus 6.4 mm, not L/175.',
 'dt.note.firestop':'The gap between floors is the path a fire uses to climb the building. A tested system fills it without stopping the slab from moving, and it has to be coordinated with insulation, the backpan and the smoke seal. It costs almost nothing to maintain and a great deal to get wrong once.',
 'dt.note.diagrid-node':'The diagonal is both the frame and the grid, so the node is where the structure, the glass bracket and the fire line all meet in one place. Chen records that the diagrid eliminates the normal column grid inside — and that eliminating it has a price, which is why the frame rate is the figure to read beside this one.',
 'dt.note.outrigger-joint':'The outrigger is the compromise: take the column away from the plate and give the floor a wider clear span, by paying for a very deep element at three or four levels. It is the one detail in this library that is entirely structural, and it is why the column-free premium is a number the model can put a price on.',
 'dt.note.parapet-coping':'Water gets behind every wall face eventually. The coping is the part that decides whether it goes down the drain or into the structure, and the counterflashing is what stops it travelling sideways into the wall instead.',
 'dt.note.plant-screen':'The enclosure is a heat exchanger. Blades cut the sun and the noise and let the equipment breathe; the free area they leave is the whole argument, and a screen specified for its look rather than its free area cooks the plant behind it.',
 'dt.note.mast-base':'The base of a post carries every load the post carries, plus wind from every direction with nothing behind it to break it. It is also the least accessible steelwork on the building, which is what the access figure is for.',
 'dt.note.aviation-mount':'A light on the highest point and on the corners of the roof, on a bracket that survives being serviced by a roped technician in a wind. Four visits a year is not optimism; it is the reason the access rate on this row is the highest in the library.',
 'dt.note.terrace-threshold':'The detail that decides whether the floor below stays dry for fifty years. Waterproofing continuity, an upstand, a drainage path and a movement joint that allows the slab and the façade to move independently — all four, or the water finds the one that is missing.',
 'dt.note.balustrade':'The most expensive safety requirement on the building per metre, because it is a guard that must not look like a guard, must not obstruct the view you paid for, and must be fixed through a waterproofing layer that was already finished.',
 'v.skyline':'{where} · rank {rank} of {total} on this plot · tallest {tall} m',
