 'r.political.h':'16 · The reading that came a year later',
 'r.political.b1':"Non Arkaraprasertkul’s 2007 MIT thesis is Shanghai contemporary: the politics of built form. Advised by Stanford Anderson and Yung Ho Chang, it studies Shanghai’s transformation through planning, politics, global markets and urban imagery. The guided reader cites its repository abstract; we do not claim it reused Chen’s cases.",
 'r.political.b2':"His 2008 Footprint paper considers urban form, individual buildings, skylines and streetscape from both pedestrian and architect-planner perspectives. It is a source for asking how a tower is experienced outside its rentable area.",
 'r.political.b3':"Our 2026 synthesis places the owner’s ledger beside access, public space, heritage and neighbourhood change. A distinctive silhouette alone cannot measure those outcomes. Evidence must include the people who occupy and use the place.",
 'r.political.b4':"The four reasons to build are alternative scenario objectives. They can reveal a trade-off, but they do not establish that symbolic ambition is profitable or publicly beneficial. Use them to state a brief, then test the brief against demand and local evidence.",
 'r.political.note':"Primary references and reading scope are linked in the four-page reader. This section’s synthesis is FormHypes, 2026.",

 'r.regime.h':'17 · Four reasons to build',
 'r.regime.b':"Chen’s 2006 system asked the architect to choose a building. Dr Non’s system also asks why. The reason is a control, and it changes which form wins. Landlord yield ranks annual contribution; city competition also rewards height and silhouette. Both use the same corrected rent accounting. A taller, stranger answer may earn more or less under the stated scenario; the result is computed, not prescribed. The city-competition reading came from towers commissioned as an answer to the last one completed: height became a scoreboard and each finished building helped set the next brief. These four reasons are FormHypes scenarios from 2026. Chen’s thesis embedded how a tower is made; it did not define landlord yield.",

 'r.limits.h':'18 · What this is not',
 'r.limits.b':'Not structural engineering. Not wind, seismic, egress or fire. Not planning code. Not BOMA measurement. Not a valuation, not NOI, and not a development appraisal — contribution is gross rent less two annual reserves, and it excludes operating cost, tax, finance and capital recovery beyond those reserves. All monetary inputs are illustrative and in USD. Every premium is a rate on a named area, and every one of them is a number you can change. Corner zones are possible footprints, not a tenancy plan. The HKIFC level schedule is a reconstruction from Chen’s legible banded-subtraction logic, not a transcript — the printed array in the scanned PDF cannot be read reliably, and we did not invent digits to fill it.',

 'r.close.h':'19 · So what',
 'r.close.b':'The thesis gave us a way to make the expertise computable. The year after gave us the reason to care what the expertise is for. Twenty years on, the useful thing is not that we can build four towers and forty variations. It is that we can now ask a client, in their own numbers, what their ambition costs — and have the answer change when they tell us what the building is for. Three moves: read the thesis, set your own rent, then read the same site under all four regimes and see which one is arguing with you.',

 'r.source':"Selected primary-source sections and original figures were inspected against the supplied MIT thesis. The guided reader provides figure/page locators and separates original work from 2026 interpretation.",

 'x.label':'Exit',
 'regime.yield.label':'Landlord yield',
 'regime.yield.reads':'Instrument',
 'regime.yield.note':'The form is a machine for turning floor area into rent. Corners pay, perimeter costs, and a plain box wins unless the premium is real.',
 'regime.competition.label':'City competition',
 'regime.competition.reads':'Flag',
 'regime.competition.note':'Height and silhouette are the ranking. The building is measured against the other towers, so the model is asked how tall and how unlike them — and does not use envelope cost in this ranking.',
 'regime.civic.label':'Civic monument',
 'regime.civic.reads':'Public gift',
 'regime.civic.note':'A public building must be legible and defensible. Form carries obligation, so distinctiveness is rewarded but it has to be paid for honestly.',
 'regime.speculative.label':'Speculative capital',
 'regime.speculative.reads':'Advertisement',
 'regime.speculative.note':'The form is the advertisement. It is bought as an image and priced as one: identity carries most of the value and envelope discipline carries least. This is the regime that produces the most aggressive towers, and the one that most needs the numbers in front of it.',
